21:The brain ' s white matter comprises densely packed myelinated axons that interconnect distinct regions of gray matter and play a critical role in healthy brain function (1,2 to white matter can disrupt coordinated neuronal activity, leading to clinical symptoms of mental illness. Accordingly, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) studies have reported diverse white matter abnormalities in different psychiatric diagnoses (3 -10).
23:These abnormalities have commonly been revealed using traditional statistical approaches that rely on comparisons of group means, which can overlook the tremendous clinical and biological interindividual variability that characterizes psychiatric illness (11 -14). Normative modeling (15,16) offers a framework for characterizing this variability by allowing one to quantify the extent to which individuals deviate on a given phenotype from normative expectations given relevant demographic characteristics such as age and sex (15 -17).
25:Normative modeling has been applied to a range of MRIderived phenotypes in different neurodegenerative and psychiatric disorders (18 -26); however, few studies have used this approach to investigate the heterogeneity of white matter deviations in psychiatric disorders. In these studies, typically no more than 20% of individuals with the same diagnosis show a signi /uniFB01 cant deviation in the same white matter location or tract (20,23,24,26). This neural heterogeneity raises an important question: how can individuals be assigned the same diagnosis if their brain deviations are so heterogeneous?
31:problem that current diagnostic criteria are not grounded in an empirical understanding of how clinical phenotypes are tied to biological mechanisms and as such do not neatly map onto the brain (27). However, patients assigned the same diagnosis often show some level of phenotypic similarity (e.g., all patients with depression, by de /uniFB01 nition, experience mood disturbances), raising the question of whether a biological correlate of such similarity can be identi /uniFB01 ed given the heterogeneity reported thus far in normative modeling studies. Accordingly, our recent work examining regional gray matter volume (GMV) deviations indicates that deviations in different anatomical locations can aggregate within common, spatially distributed brain networks in people with the same psychiatric disorder, offering a potential explanation for both phenotypic variability and similarities within and between different psychiatric diagnoses (22). In other words, heterogeneous regional GMV deviations may be related to phenotypic variability in individuals with the same diagnosis, whereas the aggregation of these deviations within common brain systems may account for phenotypic similarities.
