41:Here, we sought to extend this approach to white matter, which allows us to determine whether the extreme heterogeneity observed in GMV deviations (22) also applies to white matter volume (WMV). Psychiatric disorders are often associated with widespread disruptions of white matter connectivity, but their relationship with gray matter changes is not yet clear. Speci /uniFB01 cally, we set out to test the hypothesis that although deviations may occur in distinct regions of white matter, they nonetheless will implicate common white matter tracts and/or affect similar brain regions or extended networks. To test this hypothesis, we quanti /uniFB01 ed the anatomical heterogeneity of WMV deviations in 1465 healthy controls (HCs) and 1294 cases diagnosed with one of 6 psychiatric conditions (attention-de /uniFB01 cit/hyperactivity disorder [ADHD], autism spectrum disorder [ASD], bipolar disorder [BP], major depressive disorder [MDD], obsessive-compulsive disorder [OCD], and schizophrenia [SCZ]) (22). We developed a framework to characterize WMV deviations on multiple scales of analysis to determine the degree to which spatially heterogeneous deviations may accumulate within common white matter
