## METHODS AND MATERIALS

The study was approved by the local ethics committee of the site contributing each dataset, and written informed consent was obtained from each participant. The overall study was approved by the Monash University Research Ethics Committee (Project ID: 23534). Data processing was conducted using Multi-modal Australian ScienceS Imaging and Visualization Environment (28).

## Participants

Here, we sought to extend this approach to white matter, which allows us to determine whether the extreme heterogeneity observed in GMV deviations (22) also applies to white matter volume (WMV). Psychiatric disorders are often associated with widespread disruptions of white matter connectivity, but their relationship with gray matter changes is not yet clear. Speci /uniFB01 cally, we set out to test the hypothesis that although deviations may occur in distinct regions of white matter, they nonetheless will implicate common white matter tracts and/or affect similar brain regions or extended networks. To test this hypothesis, we quanti /uniFB01 ed the anatomical heterogeneity of WMV deviations in 1465 healthy controls (HCs) and 1294 cases diagnosed with one of 6 psychiatric conditions (attention-de /uniFB01 cit/hyperactivity disorder [ADHD], autism spectrum disorder [ASD], bipolar disorder [BP], major depressive disorder [MDD], obsessive-compulsive disorder [OCD], and schizophrenia [SCZ]) (22). We developed a framework to characterize WMV deviations on multiple scales of analysis to determine the degree to which spatially heterogeneous deviations may accumulate within common white matter

A /uniFB01 nal sample of 1294 cases and 1465 controls was obtained from a larger cohort drawn from 14 separate, independently acquired datasets and 25 scan sites following various exclusion criteria (see Supplementary Methods) (22). All cases were diagnosed according to clinical judgment and diagnostic instruments as per individual study procedures. All individuals were 18 to 64 years old. Basic demographic details of the /uniFB01 nal sample are provided in Table 1. For a comprehensive overview of inclusion criteria and demographic details for each site, see relevant references in Table 1 and Supplementary Methods.

## Analysis Overview

We characterized neural heterogeneity across various scales of network resolutions: individual WMV voxels, white matter tracts (i.e., connections between pairs of gray matter regions), brain regions, and canonical functional networks. To map WMV heterogeneity at the voxel scale, we used normative models to create person-speci /uniFB01 c voxelwise deviation maps showing how each person ' s WMV deviates from normative predictions (Figure 1A -E). This involved 1) estimating voxelwise WMV for each individual, 2) quantifying deviations from normative expectations, and 3) applying thresholds to identify deviant WMV clusters.

Next, we examined whether WMV deviations affected common axonal pathways linking similar brain regions or networks using an independent normative diffusion

