essive disorder (MDD), and schizophrenia (SCZ) at p , .05 false discovery rate (FDR) -corrected and no extreme WMV deviations with signi /uniFB01 cantly greater overlap in controls than in cases. (I) Distribution of positive ( z . 2.6; blue) and negative ( z ,2 2.6; red) deviation burden scores (i.e., the total number of extreme deviations) in each diagnostic group. * Indicates clinical groups showing a statistically signi /uniFB01 cant difference in extreme deviation burden compared with the HCtest group (Mann -Whitney U test, p , .05, 1-tailed). Steps (D -H) were subsequently repeated for positive deviations, with the deviation map thresholded at z . 2.6 to identify regions showing extreme positive deviations. BP, bipolar disorder; GMV, gray matter volume.
71:tractography dataset. This generated a dysconnectome map for each person, which identi /uniFB01 es the affected pairwise connections (Figure 2A -F). Here, the term dysconnectome refers to a map of connections that show either higher or lower WMV relative to population expectations, as indicated by positive and negative deviations from the normative model, respectively. Then we assessed whether these connections were concentrated in speci /uniFB01 c brain regions or large-scale networks (Figure 3A -E). This approach tested the hypothesis that WMV deviations may commonly affect speci /uniFB01 c axonal pathways linking brain regions or networks despite showing substantial heterogeneity in their precise anatomical location.
73:## Voxel-Scale WMV Heterogeneity
75:We estimated voxelwise WMV using voxel-based morphometry (VBM) of T1-weighted anatomical MRI scans (29,30) as per Segal et al. (22) (see Supplementary Methods). Details of the VBM procedure, image acquisition, and quality assurance procedures for the T1-weighted images are detailed in Segal et al. (22).
77:We used normative modeling to obtain person-speci /uniFB01 c WMV deviation maps relative to a model, based on Bayesian linear regression with likelihood warping (31), of population expectations for voxelwise WMV variations (see https://github. com/amarquand/PCNtoolkit, version = 0.21) (15,17). Following strati /uniFB01 cation of the sample into training (HCtrain, n = 1196) and test (clinical data, n = 1294; HCtest, n = 269) subsets (Figure 1A) [see Supplementary Methods and (22)], we /uniFB01 tted separate models to each white matter voxel to predict WMV as a function of age, sex, and site (Figure 1B) (see Supplementary Methods). Then we computed deviation zmaps for each individual (Figure 1C), which quantify the extent to which individual voxelwise WMV estimates deviate from the model prediction [see (15,16) for details; see Supplementary Methods]. Extreme deviations were de /uniFB01 ned as clusters of 10 contiguous voxels with deviation scores of | z | . 2.6 (i.e., p , .005) (22,23) (Figure 1D). See Supplementary Methods for details.
79:To characterize the interindividual heterogeneity of deviation locations, we calculated the proportion of individuals showing an extreme deviation within each white matter voxel, separately for positive and negative deviations (Figure 1E, F) (22). Then we used permutation testing to test for statistically signi /uniFB01 cant differences in the proportions of cases and controls (HCtest) showing an extreme deviation in each voxel (see Supplementary Methods for details). We repeated our analysis using various extreme deviation thresholds ( z , 2 1.96, 2 1.65) and cluster thresholds (cluster size = 50) to ensure that our /uniFB01 ndings were not driven by this speci /uniFB01 c choice.
83:The network context of WMV deviations was evaluated with respect to a normative connectome estimated in an independent, healthy, unrelated cohort of 150 diffusion-weighted MRI scans (71 male, aged 21 -35 years) from the S900 release of the Human Connectome Project (HCP150) (32). The connectome mapped 8646 tracts linking each pair of 100 cortical and 32 subcortical regions, as de /uniFB01 ned using established atlases (33,34) (see Supplementary Methods for details).
87:We produced a 132 3 132 dysconnection overlap matrix for each group using extreme deviation maps for each participant and tractograms from the HCP150 cohort. Each matrix element represents the fraction of participants whose WMV deviation intersected the tract linking regions i and j , with separate matrices for positive and negative deviations. Tract-scale heterogeneity was quanti /uniFB01 ed using a permutation-based inferential procedure similar to the analysis of voxel-scale deviations. See Figure 2 and Supplementary Methods for details.
91:To determine whether any affected tracts were preferentially linked to speci /uniFB01 c brain regions, we generated a map of gray matter regions attached to at least one affected tract in the dysconnectome matrix of each individual in the clinical and HCtest groups (Figure 3A). The resulting person-speci /uniFB01 c binary regional maps (Figure 3B), obtained separately for positive and negative deviations, were then aggregated across individuals to obtain group-speci /uniFB01 c overlap maps quantifying, at each region, the proportion of people within a given group for whom at least one affected connection was attached to that region (Figure 3E). Next, we evaluated case-control differences in overlap proportions for each region using the same procedures as those used in the voxelwise and tract-scale analyses (see the Supplement for details).
93:## Network-Scale WMV Heterogeneity
95:Next, we examined the degree to which WMV deviations aggregated within distinct functional networks. Using extreme region deviation maps (Figure 3B), we assigned each cortical region to one of 7 canonical functional cortical networks using a well-validated network parcellation (35) and assigned each subcortical region to either the medial temporal lobe (amygdala and hippocampus), thalamus, or basal ganglia (nucleus accumbens, globus pallidus, putamen, caudate nucleus), as done previously (34), resulting in a total of 10 distinct functional networks (Figure 3D).
