197:Diagnoses that are based on nosological systems such as the DSM (37) are clinical heuristics that do not necessarily de /uniFB01 ne biological entities, with several well-documented limitations including pervasive comorbidity (60) and within-group clinical heterogeneity (11). Consistent with our previous work (22), we used DSM diagnoses to evaluate heterogeneity and explore potential neural correlates of phenotypic similarities. However, as expected, this reliance may limit the degree of observed neural overlap. Longstanding therapeutic evidence indicates that treatment approaches are often agnostic to diagnostic boundaries and are rarely effective for all individuals within a diagnostic category (61 -64). Focusing on syndromes or symptoms that cut across diagnostic boundaries (65) may yield more precise mappings of voxelwise deviations and their network context and may offer important clues into the circuits to target for therapeutics (66). Similarly, determining the best way of leveraging multiscale normative modeling results to cluster individuals into subgroups is an important future extension of our work. Achieving these goals requires harmonized, transdiagnostic clinical protocols that measures diverse symptom dimensions (67 -69).
